{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261110T014351Z-be6b25ac", "ts": "2026-11-10T01:52:58Z", "actor": {"worktree": "cordyceps", "plugin": "3.8.1-dev", "branch": "feat/canvas-read", "build": "52bb14851a37"}, "body": {"base_commit": "2eb18b8c922a2edf7f0aa00be01bcf33f555cfc8", "base_tree": "ccc8644b48d75aa97017e85f99a106e6c8b6c9ef", "head_tree": "d5025318180914b23e02d538fd786ecc98b1cc3f", "head_commit": null, "dispatch_commit": "cd335d2eb61606ee63f05e3381b730c1ca4a9792", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-fable-5-2"}], "files_reviewed": ["src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasSnapshotTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectHealthTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/CanvasSnapshotTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectHealthTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-canvas-read.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Learnings Cross-Check", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no rule regressed; two rules bear on observations", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `prawduct-hook learnings-files --for-diff`: `.claude/rules/learnings/core.md` and `.claude/rules/learnings/tests.md`. No rule file changed in this interval, so there is no new-rule pass to run. Conforms: \"Code linked into `Cordyceps.Tests` must stay host-free\" (the four linked files reference no Grasshopper type and no `DebugLog`; `CanvasReader.cs` is the only host file and is not linked); \"Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate \\\"unparseable\\\" with \\\"empty\\\"\" (`ResolveMany` resolves every entry against one index and returns a per-entry result; the pure rules sit in host-free `Core/Canvas/` with tests in the same chunk); \"`int.TryParse` answers \\\"does this fit in an Int32\\\"\" (the prefix reading is chosen by shape with an ASCII-only hex test, the GUID reading is whatever `Guid.TryParse` accepts as C1.2 states, and number text is invariant with a culture test); \"Test parallelization is disabled on purpose\" (no timing test added; the generated-canvas test is seeded). Two rules are cited in observations rather than escalated, because nothing consumes the reader or snapshot in this chunk: \"A signal is only as trustworthy as what the REST of the system does to it\" (a Names-level snapshot reads as solver-on and healthy) and the silent-skip half of \"Validate-then-mutate\" (a wire whose source owner cannot be found is dropped on the same path as an infrastructure wire). No implicit supersession found in the corpus.", "files": [".claude/rules/learnings/core.md", ".claude/rules/learnings/tests.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog Reconciliation: no open item is resolved by Chunk 01", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Cache read succeeded for `brookstalley/cordyceps` (36 open items, coverage about 17 minutes old, no `sync_error`). `affecting` over the ten subject paths returned no item. Walking the open set: nothing is resolved directly or incidentally. #99 (move status, trace and the list actions onto the snapshot), #96 (batch), #101 (structured resolver errors) and #102 (outline stored values) sit downstream of this chunk and stay open; Chunk 01 builds what they need and closes none. Context for #101 only: the resolver already decides a failure kind and holds the candidate entries in its private `Outcome`, and `Describe` flattens both to text. C-B1 and C-B2: no item was created at or after the interval base (2026-11-10T01:42:11Z); #95 to #103 were filed about ten minutes before it and carry area, stage, effort and impact. C-B3: no hygiene step owed. C-B4: the added lines cite no backlog id.", "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 2}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "CanvasReader's own decisions have no automated test", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs. Why: the adapter is the one Chunk 01 file not linked into the test project (as the plan directs), but it holds rules that take plain values, not only member reads: the default-nickname comparison (nickname equals Name, or equals the proxy default), the ObjectIDs split into members and child groups with de-duplication and self-exclusion, the kind, phase and level mappings, 'a control gets no input port', and 'panel text only when the panel is unwired'. Nothing in the recorded evidence exercises any of them, and Chunks 02 and 04 build on them before the VRF-019 live check exists. The learnings rule 'New pure decision logic goes in a host-free `Core/` file linked into the test project WITH tests in the same chunk, even when it feels like host glue' and the preferences row 'host-independent logic ... is unit-tested' both point the same way. Move the value-only rules beside the snapshot (for example IsDefaultNickName(nick, name, proxyDefault) and a group split over (objectIds, nodeIds, groupIds, selfId)) with tests, and leave the adapter to copy members. Not rated as untested behaviour at the inner bar because the plan scopes the adapter as host code verified live, the same treatment DataTreeReader had in Chunk 03.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Reader relies on runtime facts the plan's unverified list does not name", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (four sites in CanvasReader.cs). Why: the plan's Requirements Confidence queues five host facts for the live check, and the reader assumes four more. Add them to VRF-019. (a) A free-floating parameter, slider or panel rests at Phase Computed after a finished solve: ToPhase folds Collected into NotComputed, so if parameter objects rest at Collected every count they own prints 'N stale' and they are counted as not solved. (b) GH_Group.Attributes.Bounds is populated for a group that has not been painted since it was created or moved; if it is empty the group's X and Y are 0 and C2's left-then-top tie-break orders groups wrongly. A host-free fallback exists (the minimum pivot of the group's effective members). (c) No parameter type reports Attributes.HasInputGrip false while holding Sources; if one does, its incoming wires vanish from the snapshot. Adding '|| param.SourceCount > 0' to the condition removes the assumption. (d) For a source owned by a component, Params.Output.IndexOf never returns -1; if it does, OutputFor reads it as output 0. The members themselves all exist in the SDK XML and are read the same way by existing code (GhCanvasTool.Values.cs for slider, toggle, value list and panel; GhInspectTool.Tree.cs for the three solver flags; ToolHelpers.GetCordycepsInfrastructureIds for the source-owner idiom), so no call is unsound as written.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Snapshot cannot tell an object that never solves from one that has not solved", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: a NodeKind.Other object that is not an IGH_ActiveObject (a scribble, a sketch) is given Phase NotComputed and Locked false by default, the same values an unsolved component carries, while PreviewHidden got a null for 'does not apply'. C2's summary field 'S not solved' and the stale qualifier read Phase, so Chunk 04 rendering from the snapshot alone would report a canvas with one scribble as '1 not solved' unless it knows to skip Kind Other, which would also skip real active objects of that kind. Make Phase nullable the way PreviewHidden is (null when the object is not an active object), or state on NodePhase that Kind Other never counts, and pin it with a snapshot test. Every other fact C2 names was found on the snapshot: document name, the three solver flags, kind, type, nickname and default flag, pivot, locked, preview, phase, level with all Error and Warning messages, control value and slider range, panel text with its truncated flag, port name, nickname, optional, modifiers, raw source count, volatile count and sources with output index, group members, child groups and position, and the derived wires, upstream, downstream, membership, parents, effective members, top-level groups and top-level groups of a node.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Resolver and prefix tests leave some acted-on outcomes unpinned", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (listed sites). Why: the plan's matrix is fully present and each failing case pins the whole message, but a few outcomes an agent would act on are reached only through the generated round-trip. (1) Text that is a unique nickname and also a prefix of two or more ids returns the prefix-ambiguity message and tells the caller to 'give more characters' for text meant as a nickname; the precedence is a literal reading of C1.4 but no test states it. (2) An ambiguous prefix in the Groups scope, and the 7-hex hint in the Groups scope. (3) Text Guid.TryParse accepts beyond the D and brace forms: the 32-hex N form, and a GUID with surrounding whitespace, which resolves while a padded nickname or prefix does not. (4) GuidPrefixIndexTests.Shortest_AgreesWithDisplayNamesShortId_OnTheSameIds fixes only the first four hex characters of each id, so with 200 ids the chance that any two share eight characters is about one in ten and the agreement is almost certainly asserted only at the 8-character minimum; zero one more byte (or reuse shared stems as ObjectResolverTests.Generate does) and assert that at least one short id is longer than 8.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "GuidPrefixIndex.Shortest and CanvasText.Number(double) have no production caller", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: IObjectIndex has no shortest-prefix member, so DisplayNames.ShortId derives the short id itself with up to 28 WithPrefix probes per object, and GuidPrefixIndex.Shortest, which the plan lists and the tests cover, is called only from tests; the two implementations are held together by the agreement test noted above. CanvasText.Number(double) is likewise tested and unused (slider values are decimals). Either route ShortId through the index (which widens the five-lookup interface C1.8 describes, so record it as a C1 amendment) or accept the duplication knowingly; drop the double overload until something calls it. The chunk's wider lack of callers (resolver, reader, CanvasReader.Health) is the plan's partition, with Chunks 02 and 04 as the named consumers, and is not raised.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Three Chunk 01 refinements are not in the plan's mid-build decisions", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the chunk was told to report contract changes rather than make them silently, and the plan's Decisions entry records the C1 refinements (group short ids in both scopes, the Groups-scope two-readings error, entries from 0, the digit in the prefix hint, group position, top-level groups of a node, cycle promotion) but not these. (1) IObjectIndex carries a sixth member, Entries, beyond the five lookups C1.8 lists; a batch overlay must keep it in step with its pending renames or 'Did you mean' and the scope hints will read pre-batch names. (2) Panel text is kept only for an unwired panel, where the deliverable says panel text without condition. (3) CanvasReader exposes Health(IGH_ActiveObject) and ToLevel for Chunk 04's ActionStatus, while the deliverable and the file's own summary say it reads a document into the model and nothing else. Add them to the same Decisions entry; none needs a code change.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Generated round-trip test sits beside a preferences row reading 'no property-based testing'", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the plan's deliverable asks for 'a property test over a generated canvas', ObjectResolverTests ships Display_EveryNameAndIdRoundTrips_OnAGeneratedCanvas, and project-preferences 'Testing strategies' reads 'table-driven via [Theory]/[InlineData]; no property-based testing'; the plan's Governing norms section does not mention that row. I read the row as descriptive (it carries no why and is not in the Enforcement table) and the test as a deterministic theory over five fixed seeds with a hand-written generator and no new dependency, so I do not rate it a norm departure. The classification is ambiguous enough that the next reviewer should not have to guess: add one line to the plan's Governing norms putting it to the owner (conforms, or a decision the owner can veto). Do not edit the preferences row to match the test.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps.Tests/ObjectResolverTests.cs"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "CanvasSnapshot.EffectiveMembers writes a cache on a type documented as immutable and usable from any thread", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: the constructor fills the cache only for top-level groups, so a later EffectiveMembers call for a nested group (or an unknown id) writes to a plain Dictionary; two threads doing that at once can corrupt it. Nothing shares a snapshot across threads today (each call builds and reads its own), so this is latent. Compute effective members for every group in the constructor and return an empty set for unknown ids without caching.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "A Names-level snapshot reads as a healthy, solver-on, unwired Full one", "recommendation": "Scope: class. One type carries two levels of completeness and only the `Detail` property tells them apart; nothing reads it (no production reference to `.Detail`). At Names detail `CanvasReader.Read` passes `new SolverFlags()` (both switches true, `IsOn` true), and every node carries defaults that are also legal real values: `Phase = NotComputed`, `Locked = false`, `MessageLevel = None`, empty `Inputs`/`Outputs`, so `Wires`, `Upstream` and `Downstream` are empty and `ObjectHealth.Classify(CanvasNode)` answers OK for every object. Who gets it wrong: Chunk 02 builds a Names snapshot per call inside the choke point, and the later wave that moves `status`/`list`/`gh_wire list` onto the snapshot will be tempted to reuse it; the result is a confident 'no errors, nothing disconnected, solver on' rather than a failure. Close it by construction, not by a list of call sites: one guard every Full-only reader passes through (for example a `RequireFull()` on the snapshot that `OutlineView`'s entry point and the snapshot overload of `ObjectHealth.Classify` call, throwing on Names), and make solver state absent on a Names snapshot instead of fabricated as on. Splitting the type (a names-only index that implements `IObjectIndex`, and the full snapshot) is the stronger form if Chunk 02 only ever needs the index. Same seam, smaller symptom: `ReadNameNode` and `ReadFullNode` assign the same seven fields by copy, so a field added to the Names level has two places to land.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "IObjectIndex has six members where C1.8 names five, and the resolver re-derives name matches from the sixth", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Contract C1 rule 8 lists the interface as lookup by GUID, entries by nickname, entries by group name, entries by prefix, and the infrastructure set; `IObjectIndex` adds `Entries` (enumerate everything). Enumeration is genuinely needed for C1.4's 'Did you mean' over the scope's names, so the member itself is defensible, but `ObjectResolver.NotFound` also uses it to answer two questions the index already answers: 'is this text a group's name' (linear scan with ordinal equality, where `index.WithGroupName(text)` is the same question) and 'is this text an object's nickname' (`index.WithNickname(text)`). That gives the batch overlay two sources of truth for name matching to keep in agreement: a decorator that overlays a rename in `WithNickname`/`WithGroupName` but returns stale entries from `Entries` resolves correctly and then prints a cross-scope hint, with an id, for a name that no longer exists. Route both hints through `WithGroupName`/`WithNickname` so `Entries` feeds suggestions only, where a stale answer is cosmetic. The interface otherwise does what C1.8 asks: every type on it is `System` or `ObjectEntry`, nothing from Grasshopper, and the `LeakyIndex` test shows the resolver's own infrastructure check holds for a careless decorator. The sixth member is not among the Chunk 01 refinements the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build' records for C1; it belongs in that list with the others so 02 and the batch wave implement the interface the contract describes.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Short id is computed two ways; the product only ever runs the slower one", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `GuidPrefixIndex.Shortest` (binary search plus neighbour comparison) and `GuidPrefixIndex.Count` have no production caller: `CanvasSnapshot` uses the index only for `StartingWith`, and every short id the product prints comes from `DisplayNames.ShortId`, which grows a prefix one character at a time through `IObjectIndex.WithPrefix`. The interface route is the right one to keep, because it is the only one a decorated index can influence, and `Shortest_AgreesWithDisplayNamesShortId_OnTheSameIds` pins the two together, so nothing is wrong today. What it costs: seven tests and a named method that read as the short-id rule while changing it changes nothing a caller sees. Either delete `Shortest` and `Count` with their tests (keeping `StartingWith`, which is live), recording that against the plan's `GuidPrefixIndexTests` deliverable since it names 'shortest unique' and 'neighbours on both sides', or accept it on the strength of the agreement test. `CanvasText.Number(double)` is in the same position (tests only; the reader formats decimals), though Chunk 04 may yet call it.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/GuidPrefixIndex.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectResolver.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/GuidPrefixIndexTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "CanvasPort.ModifierWords restates DataModifiers.MappingName", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `src/Cordyceps/Core/DataModifiers.cs` already owns the mapping-to-word table, and its doc comment calls `MappingName` 'the single source of truth' for what `modifier` and `info` emit and what `TryParseMapping` accepts back. `CanvasPort.ModifierWords` hard-codes `\"graft\"` and `\"flatten\"` a second time in a file that already imports `DataMappingChoice` from the same place. Outline will print these words next to actions that accept them back, so they are meant to be the same vocabulary; call `DataModifiers.MappingName(Mapping)` when the mapping is not `None`. Both files are already linked into the test project, so nothing else changes.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "CanvasReader caches the proxy set keyed on its count, the cache ComponentRegistry documents refusing", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `CanvasReader.DefaultNicknames` keeps a static dictionary rebuilt 'when the installed proxy count changes'. The neighbouring adapter, `ComponentRegistry.BuildCatalog`, states the opposite policy for the same data and gives the reason: 'Built fresh on each call: it is a single pass over the proxies, and a cache keyed on anything cheaper than the proxy set itself can go stale when a plugin loads.' Two adapters in `Core/` now disagree on whether the proxy set may be cached, and the new one adds mutable static state to a file whose stated job is copying values out. The practical exposure is small (a stale default-nickname flag changes which printed name an object gets, and both still round-trip), so this is about one policy rather than a defect: build the dictionary per `Read`, as the catalog does, unless a measurement on a Names read says otherwise, in which case the two adapters should share one owner of the cached proxy pass.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-13"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Snapshot is documented immutable and usable from any thread, but EffectiveMembers fills a dictionary on demand", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The class summary says the snapshot is 'immutable' and 'used afterwards from any thread'. The constructor precomputes effective members for top-level groups only; `EffectiveMembers` for any other id (a nested group asked for by `group=`, or an id that is not a group at all, which caches an empty set under a caller-supplied key) writes to the plain `_effective` dictionary at read time. No caller shares a snapshot across threads today, so nothing races. The claim is the kind others build on, though: it is what would justify Chunk 02 holding one lookup across a call's worker code, or a later wave caching a snapshot between calls. Make the claim true rather than rewording it: compute the set for every group in the constructor (the walk already exists and is seen-set guarded) and return an empty collection for unknown ids without storing it.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs"], "oid": "O-14"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two host-free models of an object's solve state now sit in sibling folders", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to two sites. `Core/Canvas` has `NodePhase` (Computed, NotComputed, Failed) plus a separate `Locked` flag, mapped from `GH_SolutionPhase` in `CanvasReader.ToPhase`; `Core/DataTrees/TreeStatusText.cs`, merged with Chunk 03 just below this interval's base, has `OwnerState` (Computed, NotComputed, Computing, Failed, Locked), mapped in `GhInspectTool.Tree.cs` `OwnerStateOf`. The two were built in parallel worktrees and the plan specified the three-value phase for the snapshot, so the code follows its spec. Low weight for this wave: outline needs only computed-or-not and locked, and `tree` is not on the list of actions to migrate onto the snapshot. It is worth one decision before Chunk 04 starts, because the snapshot's collapse of Computing into NotComputed is not recoverable later and `TreeStatusText.OwnerNote` takes the richer enum. Search `GH_SolutionPhase` under `src/Cordyceps/` to see every mapping; a single mapping that both the reader and the tree action call would end the pair.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-15"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "CanvasReader.DefaultNicknames dereferences proxy.Desc unguarded, so one bad proxy fails every read", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `CanvasReader.cs` line 260 reads `p.Desc.NickName` for every installed proxy with no null check, while every other proxy walk in the codebase guards it (`ComponentRegistry.BuildCatalog`: `if (proxy?.Desc == null) continue;`; `PluginRegistry` and the `gh_inspect` category list: `proxy?.Desc?.Category`). A null proxy or a proxy with no `Desc` throws `NullReferenceException` out of `Read` at both detail levels, and because `_defaults` is assigned only after the loop the throw repeats on every call. How it surfaces: the exception rides `ExecuteOnUiThread` back to the `McpServer` tool-invocation boundary, which logs `Tool '<name>' threw: <exception and stack>` at ERROR and returns `{success:false, error:\"Object reference not set to an instance of an object.\"}`. So the failure is detectable by an operator, but the agent gets a message it cannot act on, and once Chunk 02 lands every call that passes a nickname or prefix fails this way, plus outline. The default-nickname flag only chooses which label is printed, so it does not justify failing the read. Skip a proxy whose `Desc` is null, as the other walks do.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-16"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A throw while reading one object fails the whole snapshot and the error names no object", "recommendation": "Scope: class (every per-object read inside `CanvasReader.Read`; one wrapper at the loop closes it). `Read` has no logging and no per-object boundary. An unrecognised object type is safe: `KindOf` returns `Other`, a non-active object gets default flags and no ports, nothing throws. A top-level object with no attributes never reaches the reader (`ToolHelpers.IsActiveDocumentObject` filters it). But any getter that throws on one object (a third-party parameter's `VolatileDataCount`, `RuntimeMessages`, a slider's `Slider`) aborts the read for the entire canvas. The boundary log carries the stack, which names `ReadFullNode` or `ReadPort` but not which of several hundred objects was being read, and the caller sees only the bare exception message. The operator's only route is to bisect the canvas by hand. Catch around the per-object read in `Read` and rethrow with the object's id, type name and nickname in the message; the boundary then logs and returns something that points at the object. Do it in the reader, not the linked host-free files, which cannot call `DebugLog`.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-17"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A wire whose source owner cannot be found is dropped on the same silent path as an infrastructure wire", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. In `ReadPort`, `owner == null || !nodeIds.Contains(owner.InstanceGuid)` covers three cases with one `continue`: an infrastructure source (deliberate, per the plan), a source parameter with no attributes, and a source whose owner was filtered as orphaned. The snapshot keeps the raw `SourceCount` beside the filtered `Sources`, so the gap is visible as a difference of counts, but nothing says which case produced it and nothing is logged. An outline line would then show an input that is not DISCONNECTED and has no source, with success true. This is the pattern the learning \"Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate \\\"unparseable\\\" with \\\"empty\\\"\" calls the most common bug (a silent skip reported as success), in a read model rather than a mutation; the same null-owner skip is the convention at ten other sites, and the null-attributes case is unlikely on a live canvas, so this is low stakes. If it is worth closing, count the sources dropped for a reason other than infrastructure in the reader and write one `DebugLog` Debug line per read when the count is non-zero.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-18"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A Names-level snapshot reads as solver-on and every node as healthy, with nothing to detect the misuse", "recommendation": "Scope: class (every Full-only field read from a Names snapshot: `Solver`, `Phase`, `MessageLevel`, `Locked`, ports, `Wires`, `Upstream`/`Downstream`). At `CanvasDetail.Names` the reader passes `new SolverFlags()`, whose defaults make `IsOn` true, and nodes carry no messages and no ports, so `ObjectHealth.Classify(node)` returns OK for every node and the wire maps are empty. These are defaults standing in for values that were never read, and they are the reassuring ones. `CanvasSnapshot.Detail` records the level, but no Full-only reader checks it and `Classify(CanvasNode)` cannot. Chunk 02 will hold Names snapshots in the same call paths where Chunk 04 and the later status migration read Full ones; a snapshot handed to the wrong view produces a confident, empty, healthy answer rather than an error. The learning \"A signal is only as trustworthy as what the REST of the system does to it\" is the same shape. A construction closes it, not a list of call sites: make the Full-only surface refuse a Names snapshot (for example `Solver` null or throwing at Names, and the views taking the snapshot rather than a bare node so they can check `Detail` once).", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasSnapshot.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/ObjectHealth.cs"], "oid": "O-19"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The default-nickname cache keyed on proxy count has no recorded why and contradicts the sibling adapter's recorded reasoning", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `CanvasReader` keeps a process-wide static dictionary of default nicknames and rebuilds it only when `ObjectProxies.Count` changes. `ComponentRegistry.BuildCatalog` records the opposite decision for the same proxy set, in its doc comment: built fresh on each call because \"a cache keyed on anything cheaper than the proxy set itself can go stale when a plugin loads\". The plan's decisions do not mention the cache. The reason is probably sound (a Names read runs on every call that passes a name, and a stale flag can only change which label outline prints, because every DisplayName is checked to resolve back), but it is not written anywhere, and the next reader of the two adapters finds them disagreeing with no way to tell which is the rule. Either build the map per read like the catalog, or add the decision and its bound (label only, never resolution) to the plan's \"Decisions made mid-build\".", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/Canvas/CanvasReader.cs"], "oid": "O-20"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "scope-trace: GuidPrefixIndex.Shortest has no production caller in this chunk or any planned one", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Everything else in the chunk has a named consumer in the plan's partition (02 wires `ObjectResolver` and the Names read through `ToolHelpers`; 04 calls the Full read, `ObjectHealth`, `CanvasReader.Health` and the derived maps), so the absence of callers today is the plan's sequencing and not a gap. The exception is `GuidPrefixIndex.Shortest` (and `Count`): the production short id is computed by `DisplayNames.ShortId` through `IObjectIndex.WithPrefix`, which it must be if a batch overlay is to decorate the index, and the snapshot uses the prefix index only for `StartingWith`. `Shortest` is called from tests alone, one of which pins it equal to `ShortId`. The plan asked for its tests but names no consumer in 02, 04 or 05. Name the consumer